      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "CONTEXT",
          "content": "Recent studies reported that retinol-binding protein 4 (RBP4) has a causal role in insulin resistance and suggested that its circulating levels may predict cardiovascular disease. However, the latter assumption has not yet been tested."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "We assessed the value of RBP4 measurement in the prediction of incident coronary artery disease (CAD)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "DESIGN",
          "content": "We conducted a nested case-control study of incident CAD (n = 1036 cases vs. n = 1889 controls) selected from among 25,336 participants of the EPIC-Norfolk study."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "SETTING",
          "content": "Healthy men and women, aged between 45 and 79 yr, were recruited from age-sex registers of general practices in Norfolk."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "PATIENTS AND OTHER PARTICIPANTS",
          "content": "Participants completed a baseline questionnaire survey between 1993 and 1997, attended a clinic visit, and were followed for an average of 6 yr. Cases (n = 1036) were participants who developed CAD during the follow-up. Controls (n = 1889) matched by age, sex, and enrollment time remained free of any CAD during follow-up."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "MAIN OUTCOMES MEASURE",
          "content": "Risk of incident fatal or nonfatal CAD according to RBP4 quartiles was assessed."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "RBP4 levels were higher in cases than in controls. RBP4 levels correlated weakly with body mass index, waist-to-hip ratio, systolic and diastolic blood pressure, and total and low-density lipoprotein-cholesterol and were inversely associated with C-reactive protein concentrations. The strongest correlation was found with triglycerides. The risk of incident CAD was associated with increasing quartiles of RBP4 levels (P = 0.03). However, adjustment for cardiovascular risk factors abolished this association."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "Measurement of serum RBP4 does not provide added value for predicting CAD risk beyond traditional risk factors."
        }
      ]},
